  31: 
  32: ## Problem
  33: 
  34: `createInstallTransaction` snapshots a predicted path list. The upstream child writes
  35: elsewhere, so a failed install leaves 622 files behind while the wrapper prints
  36: `Rollback applied` unconditionally (line 1001; blocker 1,
  37: `.planning/evidence/pr69-installer-recovery-red-2026-09-14.json`). `main()` runs
  38: `cleanupV2` before the transaction exists, so the only recursive deletion has no
  39: rollback, and the old manifests are gone when line 335 reads them (blocker 3, F2).
  40: The preflight catch commits on any error (lines 965-970). Restore deletes or
  41: overwrites whatever currently sits at a path (lines 272, 277, 281).
  42: 
  43: ## Truths
  44: 
  45: 1. The upstream child is a black box and its write set moves every release. No list
  46:    stays complete. Two side-writes outside `dist/` and the manifest are known today:
  47:    `.gsd-source` and `gsd-migration-journal/` (upstream `installer-migrations.cjs`).
  48: 2. The target is shared and live. Nothing outside the roots is ever recursed into.
  49:    Measured on the owner's `~/.claude`, 2026-09-18: roots 19 MB in about 1,000 files;
  50:    `projects/` 843 MB; 62 top-level entries; twelve or more non-root directories
  51:    written continuously by other sessions; 3 of 28 top-level files changed within one
  52:    hour, one of them `.credentials.json`.
  53: 3. A rollback never deletes or overwrites owner bytes in place, including bytes that
  54:    appeared during the install window. It moves them to quarantine.
  55: 4. A rollback message is a claim. It is printed only after verification and says
  56:    exactly what was verified.
  57: 5. Every destructive step of an install happens inside the transaction, or does not
  58:    happen. `--uninstall` is outside this design except for the lock (step 12).
  59: 6. One installer at a time per target.
  60: 7. A journal at the live path means a transaction is open and its complete pre-image
  61:    exists; no journal there means no transaction is open. The journal is therefore
  62:    always written after the snapshot and deleted before it.
  63: 8. Outside the roots the installer reads top-level names only and never opens a file.
  64: 
  65: ## Names (one table in the code, three classes)
  66: 
  67: | Class | Members | Roots | Cleanup may delete | Rollback and verify |
  68: |---|---|---|---|---|
  69: | Generated | `gsd-file-manifest.json`, `.install-meta.json`, `.overlay-manifest.json`, `.gsd-profile`, `.gsd-source`, `CREDITS.md`, `gsd-install-state.json`, `package.json` | yes | yes | yes |
  70: | Legacy | `get-stuff-done/`, `get-shit-done/`, `gsd-core/` | yes | yes, recursively, in the no-manifest branch only | yes |
  71: | Observed | `settings.json`, `gsd-local-patches`, `gsd-pristine`, `gsd-migration-journal` | yes | no | yes |
  72: | Protected (wrapper-owned) | `gsd-install-transaction/`, `gsd-install-transaction-retired-*`, `gsd-install.lock`, `gsd-install.lock.stale-*`, `gsd-local-patch-history/` | no | no | rollback and verify never touch them; recognised, not reported, in the top-level check |
  73: 
  74: The Generated row replaces the inline list at lines 498-507 and the Legacy row the
  75: list at lines 482-486, so cleanup deletes nothing that is not a root with a pre-image.
  76: The containment helper refuses any manifest path under a Protected name.
  77: 
  78: ## Derived structure
  79: 
  80: 1. **Lock, then journal, then open.** `isSafeToClean` runs first, before anything is
  81:    written (so a mistyped `--config-dir` is never touched), and preflight repeats it.
  82:    Then `gsd-install.lock` is created with
  83:    `wx` and holds the pid and creation time. A lock whose pid is dead is claimed by
  84:    renaming it to `gsd-install.lock.stale-<unique>`; only the run whose rename succeeds
  85:    proceeds, and it immediately creates its own lock with `wx` (refusing if that
  86:    fails) before deleting the renamed one. The lock is released on every exit path
  87:    after acquisition: commit, rollback whether verified or incomplete, recovery, a
  88:    failed retire rename, every refusal, and both `process.exit` calls in
  89:    `uninstall()`. A lock
  90:    left by a crash is what the takeover rule is for. A pid that is alive, or
  91:    whose liveness cannot be decided, refuses with: `Another install appears to be
  92:    running (pid <pid>, lock <path>, created <time>). If no installer is running,
  93:    delete that file and run again.` Crash matrix: stale lock without journal, take
  94:    over; stale lock with journal, take over and recover (step 10); journal without
  95:    lock, recover; live lock, refuse naming the lock and any journal.
  96: 2. **Transaction directory** `gsd-install-transaction/` in the target: `snapshot/`,
  97:    `journal.json`, `quarantine/<transaction id>/new/` and
  98:    `quarantine/<transaction id>/displaced/<attempt>/`, each mirroring relative paths,
  99:    so nothing needs a suffix and a replay cannot collide.
 100: 3. **Roots.** Top-level entries of `dist/`, roots named by the existing manifests, and
 101:    the Generated, Legacy and Observed names; never a Protected name. A root that is itself a
 102:    link is refused at preflight with the root named. A link below a root is recorded
 103:    as its target string, never descended, never moved or restored; if it changed it
 104:    is reported under `Rollback incomplete`.
 105: 4. **Pre-image: copy-only and read-only.** Every pre-existing entry in the roots is
 106:    recorded: directories (including empty ones), links as above, files copied into
 107:    `snapshot/` with a SHA-256. Stored names are kept exactly; comparison keys fold
 108:    case on win32 and fold case plus NFC on darwin, and two entries folding to one key
 109:    abort. Caps: 32 MiB per file, 256 MiB total. Free space required: twice the
 110:    snapshot bytes, plus the `dist/` bytes, plus the bytes of `gsd-local-patches` and
 111:    `gsd-pristine` (the patch-history generation is a further copy), plus 64 MiB. A
 112:    cap or the free-space check failing refuses at preflight, printing the numbers and the largest entries. Any
 113:    read error aborts. The abort path deletes the incomplete snapshot and never commits.
 114: 5. **Journal.** Written once the hashed snapshot is complete and before the first
 115:    mutation. Every write, first and later, goes to a temp file in the transaction
 116:    directory and is renamed over. Fields: schema version, transaction id, wrapper
 117:    version, created and last-updated times, the pre-image index (with its time), the
 118:    top-level names taken with the pre-image, the phase, the child's pid, and the
 119:    wrapper's own expected writes. The phase becomes `spawning` before the child is
 120:    spawned and the pid is added right after, so a journal in that phase without a pid
 121:    means the child's liveness cannot be decided. The phase becomes `child-closed` on
 122:    the child's `close` event, after which its pid is never consulted. An unparseable
 123:    journal or an unknown schema version refuses with the path and the version; it is
 124:    never read as "no journal".
 125: 6. **Order inside `install()`.** Lock; journal check (step 10); preflight and
 126:    pre-image; journal; `preserveLocalPatchHistory` (reads `snapshot/`, writes the
 127:    durable `gsd-local-patch-history/` generation, journaled as an expected write);
 128:    detection and cleanup of a GSD 2.x install; child; overlay steps; commit. Everything after the
 129:    journal runs under `failWithRollback`. `main()` no longer runs cleanup.
 130: 7. **Outside the roots (names only).** One `readdir` of the target taken with the
 131:    pre-image, before cleanup, and one at verification. Names that appeared and names
 132:    that disappeared are both reported. No file outside the roots is opened; no
 133:    birthtime is consulted.
 134: 8. **On failure,** after the child's `close` event, each entry is handled by checking
 135:    its current state, so every step is idempotent: (a) the snapshot is re-hashed
 136:    against the journal index; a pre-image entry whose copy is missing or fails the
 137:    re-hash is neither displaced nor restored, its live entry stays where it is, and
 138:    it is listed under `Rollback incomplete`; (b) an
 139:    entry in a root that is absent from the pre-image moves to `quarantine/.../new/`;
 140:    nothing is deleted and the child's manifest is not consulted; (c) a pre-image entry
 141:    whose current bytes or type differ has the current entry moved to `displaced/`, then
 142:    is restored with `COPYFILE_EXCL`; on `EEXIST` the newcomer is quarantined and the
 143:    restore retried, three times at most; if the move to quarantine fails, that entry
 144:    is not restored; an entry whose bytes match but whose stored name differs only by
 145:    case is renamed back to the stored name; (d) missing pre-image files and
 146:    directories are restored. Errors (EBUSY, EPERM, EXDEV, ENOSPC) are collected per
 147:    entry, never stop the remaining entries, and never trigger copy-then-delete.
 148: 9. **Verify, then speak.** Match means: identical entry set by exact stored name and
 149:    type, identical SHA-256 for every file, identical target string for every link.
 150:    Size, mtime and ctime are never inputs. Outcomes: `Rollback applied: GSD roots
 151:    restored to their state at <pre-image time> and verified` (exit 1); `Rollback
 152:    applied: GSD roots restored to their state at <pre-image time> and verified;
 153:    top-level entries appeared or disappeared and were left untouched: <names>`
 154:    (exit 3); `Rollback incomplete`, listing what did not move or restore and why, and
 155:    one recovery instruction (exit 4). Exit 5 is a verified recovery (step 10), whatever
 156:    the top-level names did, which the message reports. Exit 6 is every refusal made
 157:    before any mutation: lock held, child possibly alive, caps or free space, a link
 158:    root, an unreadable journal, a failed retire rename. So 1 always means "failed and
 159:    rolled back" and 6 always means "nothing was touched". Amended 2026-09-19: the
 160:    outcome is `incomplete` if any entry error was collected or verification found a
 161:    mismatch, and `applied` only when both are clean; argument misuse such as `--all`
 162:    exits 2, by owner decision, which leaves both sentences true. The child's own exit code
 163:    is printed, never returned, so it cannot collide with these. Every outcome also
 164:    prints each `displaced/` entry with its quarantine path (these are bytes someone
 165:    else wrote during the window), the count of `new/` entries with the quarantine
 166:    path, and writes the full list of moved entries to `moved.txt` beside them. After
 167:    either `applied` outcome the journal is deleted first and the snapshot second
 168:    (truth 7: a kill between the two leaves a snapshot with no journal, which step 10
 169:    discards, never a journal with no pre-image), and the quarantine is kept. After `incomplete` the transaction directory is retired at once
 170:    (step 10) and the message names the retired path, so nothing loops and nothing the
 171:    message points at is deleted. A journal has no "complete" state: it exists or it
 172:    does not.
 173: 10. **A later run that finds a journal,** under the lock. Such a journal comes only
 174:     from a wrapper that was killed. If it was last updated more than 60 minutes ago,
 175:     it is retired whatever its pid says. If it is fresh and in the `spawning` phase,
 176:     and its child pid is alive or was never recorded, refuse with: `An interrupted install
 177:     from <time> may still be running (child pid <pid or unknown>). If none is, run
 178:     again after <time + 60 minutes>; the interrupted transaction will then be set
 179:     aside and kept.` Otherwise, fresh: print its date and every entry about to move,
 180:     run steps 8 and 9, print `Recovered an interrupted install from <date>` with the
 181:     step 9 listing, exit 5 asking for a re-run (exit 4 and retirement if incomplete).
 182:     Retiring: rename the transaction directory to
 183:     `gsd-install-transaction-retired-<UTC timestamp to the second>-<transaction id>`;
 184:     if that rename fails, refuse with the path and the error, mutating nothing else.
 185:     Write into it the list of root entries present now that its pre-image did not
 186:     have. Print its path, that its `snapshot/` holds the copies from before the
 187:     interrupted install, how many displaced entries its `quarantine/` holds with the
 188:     path of the `moved.txt` that lists them, and how many entries that install or
 189:     later activity left in the roots. Repeat that notice
 190:     in every outcome message of every run until the owner deletes the directory. Then
 191:     start a fresh transaction from the current state. A `snapshot/` with no journal is
 192:     deleted at preflight and reported; a quarantine with no journal is kept and named.
 193:     No flag.
 194: 11. **Commit.** Delete the journal, then `snapshot/`, in that order. Then walk every
 195:     `quarantine/*/new/` and `quarantine/*/displaced/*/` left by earlier transactions: a
 196:     quarantined file is deleted only when it is byte-identical to the file this install
 197:     placed at the same relative path; everything else stays and is named on every run. Older `before-update-*`
 198:     generations under `gsd-local-patch-history/` are pruned to one (finding F4), except
 199:     that any generation older than a retired transaction still on disk is kept, since
 200:     it is the only patch baseline taken from the true pre-install state.
 201: 12. **Uninstall** takes the same lock, releases it before both of its `process.exit`
 202:     calls, and refuses (exit 6) while a journal exists with: `An interrupted install
 203:     was found. Run the installer once to recover it or set it aside, then uninstall.
 204:     If it is older than 60 minutes, that run will also install.` Because an
 205:     incomplete rollback retires its journal at once, the only journal uninstall can
 206:     meet is a killed wrapper's, and one installer run clears it. Running uninstall
 207:     inside a transaction is tracked as issue #75, trigger: before the release that
 208:     ships this transaction.
 209: 
 210: ## Against what exists
 211: 
 212: Kept: `targetRelativePath` containment, ownership-manifest validation, the injectable
 213: seams and result shape of `install()`, the durable patch-history generation (line 753),
 214: the 17-line `overrides/bin/install.js` fix. Changed: `createInstallTransaction`,
 215: `rollbackInstallTransaction`, `commitInstallTransaction`, `copySnapshotPath`,
 216: `restoreSnapshotPath`, `preserveLocalPatchHistory` (reads the new snapshot layout),
 217: `install()` and its preflight catch, `main()`, `uninstall()` (lock only), the metadata
 218: loop (names table plus containment helper, F3), the file header (F2). Upstream's
 219: `runtime-artifact-install-plan.cjs` and its migration journal are not adopted as
 220: authority.
 221: 
 222: ## Alternatives considered
 223: 
 224: **Stage, then publish (spiked, not adopted).** Spike, 2026-09-18, owner-approved, temp
 225: directories and a fake HOME only (the real `~/.claude` fingerprint was identical before
 226: and after): the composed 1.9.1 child exited 0 and wrote 632 files, none outside the
 227: config dir. 204 of the 632 (32%) embed the absolute config-dir path, 703 occurrences,
 228: one encoding; 18 files also carry `~/` or `$HOME` forms; the child reads live state.
 229: A relocating second writer needs the same proof as the first. All review lanes agree.
 230: 
 231: **Rejected by review or by measurement:** hardlink pre-image (in-place writes share
 232: the inode); OS journals (privileged, non-deterministic); `dev`/`ino` rename tracking
 233: (subsumed by the content pre-image; both lanes concur); renaming legacy roots into the
 234: snapshot (saves about 8 MB, costs a mutation inside the pre-image); a hash-only
 235: fallback over budget (recreates the unrecoverable class); hashing top-level files and
 236: one-level entry sets outside the roots (unreachable `applied`, a release-tracking
 237: exclusion list, and the installer opening `.credentials.json`); a birthtime filter or
 238: tag (NTFS tunneling and rename hide residue); process start time in the lock (needs a
 239: subprocess per platform; the failure mode is an actionable refusal); killing the
 240: child's process tree (the wrapper has no timeout path, so the direct child is dead
 241: before any rollback. The fact first recorded for this, that the pinned installer
 242: "never requires `child_process`", was false of its require graph. The rejection now
 243: rests on a measurement, confirmed by the owner on 2026-09-19: on 1.9.1 the only spawn
 244: reached during an install is one blocking, read-only, time-bounded pid probe, which
 245: cannot write to the target and cannot outlive the child's `close` event. The guard
 246: test below is a tripwire behind that argument, not the argument. When the wrapper
 247: itself is killed nothing is left to kill a tree, so tree-killing would not cover the
 248: one case in which a descendant could matter); a
 249: `NODE_OPTIONS` preload shim (possible follow-up, not planned).
 250: 
 251: ## Blockers from `pr69-application-2026-09-13.md`
 252: 
 253: Closed by design: 1, 3. Narrowed: 2 and 4 (rollback no longer needs an ownership
 254: verdict; cleanup still does), 5 (all publication through the containment helper), 7
 255: (the snapshot moves into the transaction directory). Not addressed here: 6 patch-tree
 256: residue, 8 multi-destination modes, 9 fresh matrix and Tier S evidence.
 257: 
 258: ## Proof
 259: 
 260: `tests/acceptance/installer-recovery.cjs` becomes a gate: a package script and a CI
 261: step run it (F5). Transaction functions are Tier S: 100% branches, and each case
 262: asserts the message and exit code as well as the bytes.
 263: 
 264: Acceptance assertion (amended). The test asserted that no file remains in the target
 265: besides the owner's. Step 9 keeps the quarantine in the target, so that assertion can
 266: never pass. A blanket exemption for Protected names would be weaker than the original
 267: and would pass an implementation that deletes residue, so the replacement is an exact
 268: allowlist. Fresh-target scenario: the top level equals `owner.txt`, `settings.json`
 269: and `gsd-install-transaction`; inside that directory only `quarantine/<one id>/` with
 270: `new/**`, `displaced/**` and `moved.txt`; no lock, journal, `snapshot/`, stale lock,
 271: retired directory or `gsd-local-patch-history/`. "Exactly the residue" has an
 272: independent source: the same failure-injected child runs without the wrapper against
 273: a twin fixture, and the relative paths under `new/` must equal that inventory and be
 274: non-empty; the write trace is a lower bound only. Upgrade scenario: a successful
 275: install first, then the test hashes the whole tree with its own walker (type, SHA-256,
 276: link targets, empty directories), runs the failing install over it and requires deep
 277: equality outside the allowlist. The test never reads the wrapper's journal or snapshot
 278: to learn the pre-image. It asserts the exact outcome string ending "and verified" and
 279: `status === 1`, not merely non-zero.
 280: 
 281: Guard test (amended). Measured on the composed 1.9.1 installer, 2026-09-18 and 09-19:
 282: `dist/bin/install.js:21` requires `gsd-core/bin/lib/shell-command-projection.cjs`,
 283: which loads `node:child_process` (line 57) and spawns at lines 478, 489, 500 and 618.
 284: That one `require` is not the whole graph. In the 1.9.1 build the installer also
 285: reaches `worktree-base-ref.cjs` (`execGit` at four sites), `config-loader.cjs`
 286: (`git check-ignore` in `isGitIgnored`), and `capability-lock.cjs` (a pid start-time
 287: probe); 51 modules in that directory reference the projection module or
 288: `child_process`. Reachability across them was not argued; it was measured. One real
 289: run of the composed child, fresh install, isolated home, win32, exit 0, 632 files:
 290: one loader (`shell-command-projection.cjs`) and ONE spawn,
 291: `spawnSync('powershell', ... '(Get-Process -Id <pid>).StartTime.Ticks')` from
 292: `capability-lock.cjs` through `execTool`, 5 s timeout. It is blocking and read-only;
 293: the darwin form is `ps -p <pid> -o lstart=`. `git check-ignore` did not fire with the
 294: working directory inside a git work tree. The first proposal asserted zero spawn
 295: events and would have been red on Windows.
 296: 
 297: The guard observes instead of predicting. The real composed child runs under one
 298: preload that records, for CommonJS loads only, which module required `child_process`,
 299: `cluster` or `worker_threads` (the `node:` prefix stripped before matching), and wraps
 300: `ChildProcess.prototype.spawn`, the three sync exports, `process.binding`,
 301: `process.getBuiltinModule`, `process.dlopen` and, where present, `process.execve`,
 302: followed by `syncBuiltinESMExports()`. It captures its own `appendFileSync` first.
 303: Asserted across a fresh install, an upgrade, a failed install, and runs with the
 304: working directory inside and outside a git work tree and with a pre-existing consent
 305: lock: every observed spawn matches an allowlisted SHAPE (api, program, argument
 306: pattern, calling module), of which there are two, the `powershell` and the `ps` pid
 307: probe, both from `capability-lock.cjs`; the observed loader set, keyed by
 308: dist-relative POSIX path and case-folded on win32, is a subset of the allowlist. The
 309: "twelve bound names" assertion is dropped: a thirteenth pure helper would turn the
 310: gate red with no safety content, and a new caller of `execGit` would leave the count
 311: at twelve. Controls: the "armed" marker is written INSIDE the script-path gate, with
 312: the pid and `argv[1]`, and exactly one armed process per run is required, so a path
 313: mismatch cannot read as zero events; one record is appended per event; a missing trace
 314: is a failure. The guard is proved non-vacuous inside the real composed child, not a
 315: toy fixture: one injected event per route (`spawn`, `spawnSync`, `execFileSync`, a
 316: `require` from a module outside the allowlist, a Worker, `getBuiltinModule`) must each
 317: be caught with the right attribution. A failure message names this section, so the
 318: allowlist is not simply edited. The failed-install run shares one child with the
 319: acceptance scenario. Residual risk recorded: paths these runs do not exercise; native
 320: addons and other non-JavaScript routes; descendants of an allowed probe; loader
 321: attribution for ESM `import()` (the spawn wraps still catch what it does); and Bun,
 322: since the child is started with `process.execPath` and a source scan for `Bun.spawn`
 323: does not cover Bun's `node:child_process`. A bump that adds a spawn shape or a loader
 324: fails the gate and reopens the process-tree decision.
 325: 
 326: Adversarial cases: owner write inside a root during the window; a `settings.json`
 327: write landing between quarantine and restore; concurrent `settings.json` edit
 328: surviving a restore; same-size same-mtime rewrite detected; child deletes a
 329: pre-existing file and an empty directory; file replaced by a directory; case-only
 330: rename (named skip on Linux); two names folding to one key; link root refused; link
 331: below a root recorded and never descended; child side-write to a new top-level name;
 332: another session's new top-level entry; second failure against an existing quarantine;
 333: GSD-named quarantined file with owner content surviving the prune; two installers
 334: against the lock; stale-lock takeover race; live-pid refusal message; wrapper killed
 335: mid-child and mid-rollback, recovered by the next run; replay of a half-finished
 336: recovery; stale journal retired with intervening owner edits untouched; journal
 337: torn, unparseable and unknown-schema; orphaned child alive at recovery; snapshot hash
 338: mismatch; the child run against a populated transaction directory leaving it
 339: byte-identical; manifest path under a Protected name refused; caps and free-space
 340: refusal before mutation; EPERM during pre-image aborting with no commit; ENOSPC through
 341: the fs seam during snapshot, quarantine creation and restore; a locked file held by a
 342: PowerShell helper with `FileShare.None`; child exit by signal with a null code;
 343: uninstall refused by the lock and by a journal, with its instruction; a no-manifest
 344: target whose `get-shit-done/` is deleted by cleanup and restored by rollback; a
 345: mistyped config dir left without a lock file; the lock released on every exit path
 346: including both uninstall exits; a child exiting 3 or 4 not changing the wrapper's
 347: code; a top-level entry that disappears during the window; a case-only rename restored
 348: to the stored name; a journal in the `spawning` phase with no pid refused with its
 349: instruction; a stale journal whose pid now belongs to an unrelated live process still
 350: retired; an incomplete rollback retired at once with the message naming the retired
 351: path; a patch-history generation older than a retired transaction surviving the prune;
 352: displaced entries printed in every outcome and `moved.txt` written; the wrapper killed
 353: between deleting the journal and deleting the snapshot, the next run discarding the
 354: snapshot and touching nothing else; a snapshot copy that fails its re-hash leaving the
 355: live entry in place and reported; every refusal exiting 6 with the target
 356: byte-identical; a verified recovery exiting 5 when top-level names changed; a journal
 357: in the `child-closed` phase recovered although its pid is reused by a live process;
 358: a journal dated in the future retired whatever its pid says, including the sequence
 359: wrapper killed, child alive, clock stepped back, which pins the owner's round-four
 360: choice; argument misuse exiting 2 with the target byte-identical; a manifest entry
 361: whose first segment is neither shipped nor known refused with the entry named; a
 362: source file rewritten during its snapshot copy aborting as a read error; a unit move
 363: failing with `EBUSY` falling back to per-entry moves.
 364: One mutation check per gate: force the
 365: verify comparison to always-equal and assert the acceptance test goes red. Amended:
 366: against a clean rollback that mutant survives, because verification would have passed
 367: anyway. A forced restore ERROR does not kill it either, because a collected error
 368: already yields `incomplete` whatever the comparison returns (the outcome rule, now in
 369: step 9). The mutant is killed by a rollback that reports no error and is still wrong:
 370: a preload gated on the wrapper's script path lets the restore copy of one named file
 371: succeed and then appends a byte to the destination. Correct code reports `Rollback
 372: incomplete`, exits 4 and retires the directory; the mutant reports `applied` and exits
 373: 1. It runs identically on the three operating systems, with no file lock and no
 374: timing. The forced-error scenario is kept as a separate case for error collection:
 375: the same preload throws `EPERM` for one destination on every copy primitive the
 376: restore could use, and the test asserts the injection was reached. No `chmod` (CI
 377: containers run as root); the PowerShell `FileShare.None` helper stays in the
 378: adversarial list and out of the gate. The mutant is a copy of the transaction module
 379: with one exact text replacement whose match count is asserted to be one, reached
 380: through a wrapper-path override that lives in the test; no mutation switch exists in
 381: Tier S product code.
