# Quality Standards Implementation Guide

Version: 1.1. Updated: 2026-09-05.
Canonical policy: `~/.claude/rules/quality-standards.md`. Read it first.
This SKILL.md is the shared authoring file; the Codex-discovered copy is a
symbolic link. Edit the canonical file in place; verify link integrity after
any installer or tool replaces files.

## Required enforcement

1. Inventory executable files and classify every file by the policy's risk
   tiers. Enforce statements, branches, functions and lines separately for
   each applicable file AND package. Tier S requires 100% branches plus
   adversarial boundary tests and mutation or equivalent decision checks;
   its other metrics retain the 95% minimum. Higher project bars remain valid.
2. Include files that tests never import. Reject missing or malformed evidence,
   including absent files/metrics, nonfinite percentages and values outside
   0..100. A zero denominator needs an explained applicability decision rather
   than an automatic claim that the behavior is tested.
3. Use individual reviewed exclusions with a reason. Do not exclude entrypoints,
   barrel files, schemas or generated code by name/glob: inspect their actual
   runtime behavior. No 90% error-path or 85% edge-case exceptions exist.
4. Enforce changed-line coverage at the file's tier. Whole-file or package
   totals cannot excuse an untested change.
5. Negative-test the gate: a passing package with one failing critical file,
   a Tier S file at 99% branches, an unregistered executable file, missing or
   invalid report data, and a deliberately incorrect trust-boundary decision
   must fail. Use real instrumented execution as well as parser fixtures.

## Tool configuration

Use the project's installed instrumentation and machine-readable reports.
A global Jest coverageThreshold, pytest --cov-fail-under, shell statement
coverage, or a formatted-text grep alone does not establish this contract.
Check the installed tool's metric and per-file semantics before configuring it.
Where a language tool cannot measure a required metric, record the limitation
and use a reviewed explicit alternative; do not fabricate a percentage.

For JavaScript/Bun, preserve real relative imports when instrumenting fixtures,
include unimported source files in the registry, validate each file's numeric
summary, and enforce package totals separately. Conversations provides a
working reference in `C:/Projects/conversations/scripts/coverage-gate.cjs` and
`scripts/sync-to-desktop-coverage.cjs`; its remaining legacy exception and
incomplete whole-project inventory are not a reusable compliance claim.

## Delivery layers

Prefer Bun for project commands where supported. Reuse the existing project
quality command locally and in CI. Local hooks give feedback; required CI and
final-revision review own the merge boundary. A skipped or partial quick check
cannot substitute for the full gate. Inspect aidev release/publish's actual
checks before claiming it enforces every tier; do not infer capabilities from
its command name or a green aggregate message.

Require attributable review evidence for judgment-based requirements, expose
missing review, and avoid brittle checks for subjective style. Never bypass
or weaken gates to manufacture a pass. Reviewed existing exceptions remain
visible with owner and concrete retirement trigger.

## Functional completion

Prove implementation, wiring, invocation, useful outcome and failure/recovery.
Use focused behavioral and adversarial tests, real integration checks and
operational evidence appropriate to the change. Coverage supplements these
proofs. Refactor side effects behind injectable boundaries when needed; do not
write tests that merely restate the implementation to inflate percentages.

